I'll write about the quiet, often overlooked power of boredom.

In a world saturated with constant stimulation—notifications, feeds, and endless entertainment—boredom has become almost taboo. Yet it’s in those unstructured moments, when the mind has nothing to latch onto, that creativity often sparks. Without the pressure to perform or consume, the brain wanders, making unexpected connections.

Historically, many breakthroughs emerged from idle time: a walk, a long commute, or simply staring out a window. Boredom isn’t a void to be filled; it’s a space where the mind reorganizes itself. Reclaiming even a few minutes of it daily might be one of the simplest ways to think more clearly. [end of text]


